<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- Chapter 3 exercises.

     One flat list, numbered 1-4 in order. Which exercises get assigned
     together lives in the course schedule, not here.

     Add more by appending another <exercise> below; numbering of the
     existing ones will not shift. -->

<exercises xml:id="ch3-exercises">
  <title>Exercises</title>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="hw-nash-definition">
    <title>The Nash Equilibrium Definition</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Suppose we have a game with <m>N</m> players (<m>N \geq 3</m>), and
        let <m>s^* = (s_1^*, s_2^*, \ldots, s_N^*)</m> be a strategy profile.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="hw-nash-definition-math">
      <statement>
        <p>
          State, mathematically, what it means for <m>s^*</m> to be a Nash
          equilibrium. Your statement must be written in terms of the
          payoff functions <m>\pi_i</m>, using notation for an
          <m>N</m>-player game.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-nash-definition-not">
      <statement>
        <p>
          State, mathematically, what it means for <m>s^*</m> to
          <em>not</em> be a Nash equilibrium, again in terms of the payoff
          functions <m>\pi_i</m>. Then explain in words what your
          statement says.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-nash-definition-matrix">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Consider the two player game below. Player 1 chooses a row from
          <m>S_1 = \{a, b, c\}</m> and player 2 chooses a column from
          <m>S_2 = \{x, y, z\}</m>, with payoffs listed in the order
          (player 1, player 2).
        </p>

        <table xml:id="table-hw-three-profiles">
          <title>A <m>3 \times 3</m> game</title>
          <tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
            <col halign="left" right="minor" />
            <col right="minor" />
            <col right="minor" />
            <col />
            <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
              <cell></cell>
              <cell>Player 2: <m>x</m></cell>
              <cell>Player 2: <m>y</m></cell>
              <cell>Player 2: <m>z</m></cell>
            </row>
            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell>Player 1: <m>a</m></cell>
              <cell>(4, 3)</cell>
              <cell>(0, 1)</cell>
              <cell>(1, 2)</cell>
            </row>
            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell>Player 1: <m>b</m></cell>
              <cell>(2, 0)</cell>
              <cell>(5, 5)</cell>
              <cell>(0, 6)</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Player 1: <m>c</m></cell>
              <cell>(1, 3)</cell>
              <cell>(6, 0)</cell>
              <cell>(3, 3)</cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>
          For each of the following strategy profiles, determine whether it
          is a Nash equilibrium, justifying your answer with payoff
          comparisons. If it is a Nash equilibrium, say whether it is
          strict.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><p><m>(a, x)</m></p></li>
          <li><p><m>(b, y)</m></p></li>
          <li><p><m>(c, z)</m></p></li>
        </ul>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="hw-water-pollution-five">
    <title>Water Pollution with Five Firms</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        This is a larger version of the Water Pollution game from
        <xref ref="read-3-3" text="title" />. Five firms share a lake.
        Each firm chooses to <term>purify</term> its wastewater, at a cost
        of 1, or to <term>pollute</term>, at no cost. If 3 or more firms
        pollute, the lake is fouled and <em>every</em> firm pays an
        additional cleanup cost of 3. A firm's payoff is the negative of
        its total cost. For example:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            If only firm 1 pollutes, the lake stays clean. Firm 1 gets
            <m>0</m>, and firms 2 through 5 each get <m>-1</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            If firms 1 through 4 pollute, the lake is fouled. Firms 1
            through 4 each get <m>-3</m>, and firm 5 gets
            <m>-1 - 3 = -4</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        There are <m>2^5 = 32</m> strategy profiles, but you should not
        check them one at a time. Since all firms are treated the same, the
        analysis of a profile depends only on how many firms pollute.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="hw-water-pollution-five-k">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>k</m> be the number of polluting firms. For each
          <m>k = 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5</m>, determine whether the profiles with
          exactly <m>k</m> polluters are Nash equilibria. Justify each
          answer.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-water-pollution-five-all">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Describe all Nash equilibria of the game, and determine how many
          there are.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="hw-bank-location-nash">
    <title>Locating Two Banks, Revisited</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Return to the two competing banks of
        <xref ref="hw-bank-location" />: each bank chooses a block
        <m>s_i \in \{1, 2, \ldots, 99\}</m> along a 99-block avenue, each
        block has 100 residents, residents use the closer bank, and a block
        equidistant from both banks splits its residents evenly. The payoff
        to each bank is its total number of customers. In that exercise we
        used iterated elimination to show that <m>(50, 50)</m> is a
        dominant strategy equilibrium.
      </p>

      <p>
        As before, please do not write out a <m>99 \times 99</m> matrix.
        Fix one bank's location and think about how the other bank's
        payoff changes as it moves.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="hw-bank-location-nash-center">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show directly, without using iterated elimination, that
          <m>(50, 50)</m> is a Nash equilibrium.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-bank-location-nash-apart">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show that any strategy profile <m>(p, q)</m> where
          <m>p \lt q</m> is not a Nash equilibrium.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          Split into two cases: the banks are not adjacent
          (<m>q \geq p + 2</m>), and the banks are adjacent
          (<m>q = p + 1</m>).
        </p>
      </hint>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-bank-location-nash-same">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show that any strategy profile <m>(p, q)</m> where
          <m>p = q \neq 50</m> is not a Nash equilibrium.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="hw-resource-game">
    <title>Bidding the Resource</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Each of two players has 1 (divisible) unit of a resource. Player 1
        chooses some amount <m>s_1 \in [0, 1]</m>, and player 2
        simultaneously chooses some amount <m>s_2 \in [0, 1]</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        The player that chooses the higher amount wins the total of what
        remains, <m>(1 - s_1) + (1 - s_2)</m>. The player that chooses the
        lower amount gets a payoff of zero, forfeiting the remaining amount
        to the other player. In the case of a tie, they split what remains.
        The payoffs are:
      </p>

      <me>
        \pi_1(s_1, s_2) =
        \begin{cases}
          (1 - s_1) + (1 - s_2), \amp s_1 \gt s_2 \\
          1 - s_1, \amp s_1 = s_2 \\
          0, \amp s_1 \lt s_2
        \end{cases}
      </me>
      <me>
        \pi_2(s_1, s_2) =
        \begin{cases}
          (1 - s_1) + (1 - s_2), \amp s_2 \gt s_1 \\
          1 - s_2, \amp s_1 = s_2 \\
          0, \amp s_2 \lt s_1
        \end{cases}
      </me>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="hw-resource-game-one-one">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show that <m>s_1 = s_2 = 1</m> is a Nash equilibrium. Is it a
          strict Nash equilibrium? Explain.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-resource-game-below-one">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show that there are no Nash equilibria where
          <m>s_2 \lt s_1 \lt 1</m>, by showing that player 2 can improve.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-resource-game-at-one">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Now suppose <m>s_2 \lt s_1 = 1</m>. Explain why player 2's
          deviation from part (b) no longer works, and show that the
          profile is still not a Nash equilibrium.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-resource-game-tie">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show that there are no Nash equilibria where
          <m>s_1 = s_2 \lt 1</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <conclusion>
      <p>
        <alert>Remark.</alert> There are infinitely many strategy profiles
        in this game, and parts (a) through (d) break them into exactly
        four cases. The cases we did not explicitly consider are handled
        through symmetry. For example, part (b) also shows there are no
        Nash equilibria where <m>s_1 \lt s_2 \lt 1</m>. So <m>(1, 1)</m>
        is the only Nash equilibrium of this game.
      </p>
    </conclusion>
  </exercise>

<interactive xml:id="question-form-3-ex" iframe="question-form.html?section=chapter 3 exercises" width="100%" aspect="2:1"/>
</exercises>
